---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { href } from '../lib/paths'

const enforced = [
  ['Container jobs only.', 'In host mode the runner is configured to reject any job without a container: line. If OwnCI can’t establish this, it refuses to start the runner.'],
  ['The runner stays in its container.', 'In container-runner mode the runner is only ever started inside its container, which is replaced after every job. The cleanup hook is mounted read-only, so a job can’t switch the wipe off.'],
  ['A clean workspace for every job.', 'After each job, _work (workspace, actions, tools) is wiped from a throwaway, network-less container using a digest-pinned image. If the cleanup fails, the job fails visibly.'],
  ['Opt-in label.', 'The runner carries only the ownci label, so a stray runs-on: self-hosted never lands on your laptop.'],
  ['No privileged containers.', 'None of the containers OwnCI uses are privileged.'],
  ['Tokens handled carefully.', 'Your GitHub token is never stored and the registration token is single-use. Runner credentials live in directories with mode 0700 and files with mode 0600, and tokens are redacted from the logs OwnCI writes and captures.'],
  ['Checked every time.', 'All of this is re-asserted on every start and reported by status and doctor.'],
]

const limits = [
  ['The Docker socket is mounted into job containers.', 'The official runner does this, and jobs run as root. A job can therefore control your Docker daemon, which is effectively root on your machine. The container is a barrier against accidents, not against a malicious workflow.'],
  ['Network.', 'Job containers use Docker’s default network and can reach the internet and your LAN. OwnCI sets no network policy.'],
  ['Sibling containers.', 'services:, uses: docker://… and Dockerfile actions run as siblings on your Docker daemon, outside the job container and outside OwnCI’s control.'],
  ['Shared Docker daemon.', 'Jobs share the daemon, images and build cache with everything else on the machine. Images persist between jobs; only _work is wiped.'],
  ['Root in the container.', 'Containers run as root by default and can read and write the runner’s work directories while a job runs.'],
  ['The runner runs on your host as you.', 'OwnCI only allows container jobs, but a vulnerability in the runner, Docker or an image is a vulnerability on your machine.'],
  ['No resource limits.', 'No CPU, memory or disk limits are applied to jobs.'],
  ['Container-runner credentials.', 'In container-runner mode a job without container: runs next to the runner’s registration credentials and can read or change them while it runs.'],
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security and trust model · OwnCI"
  description="What OwnCI enforces on your machine, and what a Docker container does not protect you from. OwnCI is built for private repositories whose workflows you trust."
>
  <main id="main" class="content-page">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-dot"></span> Security and trust model</p>
      <h1>Built for code you trust.<br /><span>Honest about the rest.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        OwnCI runs code from your workflows on your machine. Here is exactly what it enforces, and
        what a container does not protect you from. Read this before pointing it at anything you
        don’t control.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="split-section" aria-labelledby="supported-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">Supported</p>
        <h2 id="supported-title">Private repositories, trusted workflows.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          OwnCI is meant for <em>private</em> repositories whose workflows you trust: your own, your
          team’s. <code>ownci setup</code> <strong>refuses public repositories</strong> unless you
          pass <code>--allow-public-repo</code>, because pull requests from forks can run arbitrary
          code on a self-hosted runner.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you accept that risk anyway, set the repository’s <em>Fork pull request workflows from
          outside collaborators</em> to <em>Require approval for all outside collaborators</em>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="enforced-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">What OwnCI enforces</p>
        <h2 id="enforced-title">Guard rails that are checked, not assumed.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="honest-list">
        {enforced.map(([title, body]) => (
          <li><span class="mark yes" aria-hidden="true">✓</span><span><strong>{title}</strong> {body}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">What a container does not protect against</p>
        <h2 id="limits-title">A container is not a sandbox for hostile code.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="honest-list">
        {limits.map(([title, body]) => (
          <li><span class="mark no" aria-hidden="true">!</span><span><strong>{title}</strong> {body}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="callout warn"><strong>OwnCI does not claim to safely run arbitrary untrusted pull request code.</strong></p>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <span class="cta-spark" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>
      <div><p class="section-kicker">Comfortable with the model?</p><h2>Set up your first runner.</h2><p>Start with <code>ownci doctor</code>; it checks isolation too.</p></div>
      <a class="button-primary" href={href('/docs/')}>Get started <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
